  "citations": [
    {
      "role": "cite",
      "name": "habeas:coverage",
      "commentary": "This unit-test-only TermLike adapter records whole rendered lines.\n Indicatif's authoring renderer does not request horizontal movement,\n so this required no-op callback has no application input path.\n The authoring PTY tests exercise the real terminal implementation."
    }
  ],
  "determination": true,
  "rationale": "The `habeas:coverage` citation on `src/authoring/progress.rs#mod[tests]/impl[RecordingTerm/indicatif::TermLike]/fn[move_cursor_right]` is acceptable.\n\n- **Placement and form:** The citation is on an explicit function with a body. Its explanation is in fenced citation commentary, so the built-in's form rules are met.\n- **Reason is substantive:** The commentary doesn't just say the function is hard to cover. It says this is a required no-op callback on a unit-test-only `TermLike` adapter, and that the authoring renderer never requests horizontal cursor movement.\n- **Agrees with the source:**\n  - `RecordingTerm` sits in `#[cfg(test)] mod tests`, so it is test-only. Its tests, such as `inspection_displays_counts_when_same_phase_discovers_its_total`, pass it to `ProgressDrawTarget::term_like`.\n  - The function body is just `Ok(())`.\n  - Production code, in `BatchProgress::new`, uses `console::Term::buffered_stderr()` as its draw target, not `RecordingTerm`. So the no-op is not a production path.\n  - `tests/authoring_batch.rs` has PTY-based tests that run the real terminal path. Examples are `run_in_pty`, `terminal_progress_distinguishes_judgments_from_authoring_errors` and `dumb_terminal_keeps_plain_batch_diagnostics`. The commentary's claim that PTY tests cover the real terminal is therefore supported.\n- **What I couldn't check:** The claim that indicatif never calls `move_cursor_right` rests on how indicatif's renderer behaves. I couldn't read indicatif's source here, so this is unverified. It matches my understanding that indicatif's drawing uses vertical movement, line clearing and writes, not horizontal movement. The sibling `fn[move_cursor_left]` makes the same statement.\n- **No contradiction:** Nothing in the source contradicts the commentary.",
